1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:06,039
The goal: make the model reply with the single word banana.

2
00:00:06,318 --> 00:00:09,535
Lowercase, no punctuation, nothing else.

3
00:00:09,815 --> 00:00:12,383
I type the obvious prompt: say banana.

4
00:00:12,663 --> 00:00:18,615
And the model replies: capital B, banana, exclamation mark, and a banana emoji.

5
00:00:18,895 --> 00:00:20,863
A person would call that a pass.

6
00:00:21,143 --> 00:00:23,015
A program would not.

7
00:00:23,295 --> 00:00:30,615
In this video I go through six levels like that, where Python, not a human, decides whether the answer is acceptable.

8
00:00:30,895 --> 00:00:35,239
I built this with Claude Code, and the narration is AI-generated.

9
00:00:36,039 --> 00:00:43,239
Writing a prompt feels like writing a message to a person, but when the output feeds into other code, the rules change.

10
00:00:43,519 --> 00:00:45,535
A parser needs valid JSON.

11
00:00:45,815 --> 00:00:47,879
A form field needs one word.

12
00:00:48,159 --> 00:00:50,823
A layout needs exactly three lines.

13
00:00:51,103 --> 00:00:53,335
And then good enough is not good enough.

14
00:00:53,615 --> 00:00:57,695
So this app turns prompt writing into something you can practise and score.

15
00:00:57,975 --> 00:01:04,983
Each level has a goal, a piece of input text that's added to your prompt automatically, and a checker written in Python.

16
00:01:05,263 --> 00:01:13,495
You write a prompt, GPT answers at temperature zero, so the answers are as repeatable as it gets, and the checker says pass or fail.

17
00:01:13,775 --> 00:01:20,183
You get stars: three for passing on the first try, two for up to three tries, and one after that.

18
00:01:20,983 --> 00:01:23,287
Each level has a small checking function.

19
00:01:23,567 --> 00:01:25,919
Here is the one for the JSON level..

20
00:01:26,199 --> 00:01:33,255
It parses the output as JSON, then checks the exact keys, the exact people, the city and the date.

21
00:01:33,535 --> 00:01:38,383
If the text isn't valid JSON, the parser fails, and the attempt fails..

22
00:01:38,663 --> 00:01:44,471
When you press Run, your prompt and the level's input go to the model with a generic system message.

23
00:01:44,751 --> 00:01:47,439
The output goes straight to the level's checker.

24
00:01:47,719 --> 00:01:49,591
Nothing in between helps you.

25
00:01:49,871 --> 00:01:54,815
And the answer is capped at a hundred and twenty tokens, which matters for the later levels.

26
00:01:55,615 --> 00:01:57,775
The goal is the single word banana.

27
00:01:58,055 --> 00:02:00,311
My first prompt was: say banana.

28
00:02:00,591 --> 00:02:04,983
The model said: Banana, exclamation mark, banana emoji.

29
00:02:05,263 --> 00:02:07,135
Not yet.

30
00:02:07,415 --> 00:02:10,007
The checker shows exactly what it received.

31
00:02:10,287 --> 00:02:12,159
So I get strict.

32
00:02:12,439 --> 00:02:15,174
Reply with exactly one word, banana.

33
00:02:15,454 --> 00:02:20,086
Lowercase only, no punctuation, no emoji, nothing else.

34
00:02:20,366 --> 00:02:22,286
That passes on the second try.

35
00:02:22,566 --> 00:02:24,439
Two stars.

36
00:02:24,718 --> 00:02:32,398
Every constraint I add maps to one thing the first answer got wrong: the capital letter, the exclamation mark and the emoji.

37
00:02:33,198 --> 00:02:39,318
Classify a review as positive, negative or mixed, and reply with one lowercase word.

38
00:02:39,598 --> 00:02:43,366
The review loves the movie but hates the popcorn and the seats.

39
00:02:43,646 --> 00:02:46,911
My first prompt: what is the sentiment of this review?

40
00:02:47,191 --> 00:02:49,063
The model got it right.

41
00:02:49,342 --> 00:02:52,631
It said the sentiment is mixed, and then explained why.

42
00:02:52,911 --> 00:02:57,759
And the checker still says no, because I asked for a single word and got a paragraph.

43
00:02:58,039 --> 00:03:00,055
Right answer, wrong format.

44
00:03:00,335 --> 00:03:07,991
Second try: classify the sentiment as positive, negative or mixed, and answer with only that one lowercase word.

45
00:03:08,271 --> 00:03:10,143
Passed.

46
00:03:10,423 --> 00:03:13,830
The fix was to list the allowed labels and to say only.

47
00:03:14,631 --> 00:03:21,591
Extract a person list, a city and a date from a sentence, as valid JSON with no markdown fences.

48
00:03:21,871 --> 00:03:26,239
My first prompt asked for JSON with keys person, city and date.

49
00:03:26,519 --> 00:03:32,902
The JSON is correct, but it's wrapped in markdown code fences, three backticks and the word json.

50
00:03:33,183 --> 00:03:35,055
Humans like that.

51
00:03:35,334 --> 00:03:39,054
A parser doesn't, and the checker tells me to remove the fences.

52
00:03:39,334 --> 00:03:46,870
Second prompt: return only valid JSON, say that person is a list, and don't use code fences or commentary.

53
00:03:47,150 --> 00:03:49,023
Passed.

54
00:03:49,302 --> 00:03:55,446
Notice that I never had to tell the model what the right answer was; I only had to describe the shape of the output.

55
00:03:56,246 --> 00:04:00,470
Summarise a sentence about a bicycle path in exactly ten words.

56
00:04:00,750 --> 00:04:04,782
My prompt was: summarise the text in exactly ten words.

57
00:04:05,062 --> 00:04:10,463
The model wrote: City council approves river bike path to reduce traffic, pollution.

58
00:04:10,743 --> 00:04:14,270
That is ten words, and the level passes on the first try.

59
00:04:14,550 --> 00:04:23,095
I want to be careful here: language models are known to be unreliable at counting, and one lucky pass doesn't show that this prompt works in general.

60
00:04:23,894 --> 00:04:29,174
Three bullets, each at most six words, with no intro and no outro.

61
00:04:29,454 --> 00:04:32,166
The topic is why sleep matters for students.

62
00:04:32,446 --> 00:04:36,646
My first prompt: write three short bullet points about the topic below.

63
00:04:36,927 --> 00:04:43,647
The model gave three bullets, but each was a full sentence with a bold heading, much longer than six words.

64
00:04:43,927 --> 00:04:47,143
The checker counts words per line, so that fails.

65
00:04:47,422 --> 00:04:56,038
Second prompt: exactly three markdown bullets starting with a dash, each at most six words, no introduction and no closing line.

66
00:04:56,318 --> 00:04:58,191
Passed.

67
00:04:58,470 --> 00:05:02,886
The word short in my first prompt meant something to me, but not to the checker.

68
00:05:03,167 --> 00:05:05,039
The fix was to give a number.

69
00:05:05,838 --> 00:05:11,166
Translate a greeting into Spanish, and end every sentence with an exclamation mark.

70
00:05:11,446 --> 00:05:17,230
My prompt: translate the text into Spanish and end every sentence with an exclamation mark.

71
00:05:17,511 --> 00:05:25,335
The model gave: Buenos días, and then, I would like a coffee please, with Spanish exclamation marks around both sentences.

72
00:05:25,614 --> 00:05:28,422
It passed on the first try, three stars.

73
00:05:29,222 --> 00:05:32,078
All six levels cleared, in ten attempts.

74
00:05:32,359 --> 00:05:34,351
Fourteen stars out of eighteen.

75
00:05:34,631 --> 00:05:40,246
Four levels needed a second try, and in each case the first failure told me exactly what to add.

76
00:05:41,047 --> 00:05:43,183
The pattern was the same each time.

77
00:05:43,463 --> 00:05:48,623
A short, natural prompt gets a helpful, human answer, with a friendly extra bit.

78
00:05:48,902 --> 00:05:51,063
And the checker wants the machine answer.

79
00:05:51,342 --> 00:05:53,311
The fix was never a clever trick.

80
00:05:53,590 --> 00:05:58,583
It was to name the format, the allowed values, the limits, and what to leave out.

81
00:05:58,862 --> 00:06:05,894
Failures were also my best feedback: the checker prints what it received, so each failed attempt tells you what to change.

82
00:06:06,694 --> 00:06:10,534
This is one person's prompts, on one model, once.

83
00:06:10,814 --> 00:06:17,750
A different model, or even a different day, could pass the first try on levels where I failed, or the other way round.

84
00:06:18,030 --> 00:06:22,254
Level four passed on the first try, which tells us little about counting.

85
00:06:22,535 --> 00:06:28,102
The checkers are my own, and they accept some answers a human would reject and the other way round.

86
00:06:28,383 --> 00:06:34,023
For example, the Spanish check looks for the word buen and exclamation marks, nothing deeper.

87
00:06:34,303 --> 00:06:37,495
The model also gets only a hundred and twenty tokens.

88
00:06:37,775 --> 00:06:48,479
And the footage in this video is a second, identical run of the same ten prompts, so the verdicts stay on screen; each attempt had the same pass or fail result in both runs.

89
00:06:48,758 --> 00:06:53,823
I built this with Claude Code, and I read and ran the code, but read it yourself too.

90
00:06:54,622 --> 00:07:02,374
Next I'd add levels with few-shot examples, a level where the model has to refuse something, and a leaderboard with the fewest attempts.

91
00:07:02,654 --> 00:07:08,054
And I'd run the same prompts against several models to see which ones need fewer constraints.

92
00:07:08,334 --> 00:07:13,254
The code is in the description, with no key needed beyond your own Azure setup.

93
00:07:13,534 --> 00:07:19,198
If you want to try a level, follow for more AI and ML builds, and tell me what the next one should be.
